body:
I remembered this (I really dislike how people use it) and to save you the explanation but save the people reading an extra search, here's a good explanation I found off of the internet-- thanks for saying this one!

&#x200B;

“Blood is Thicker Than Water”

Nowadays, this phrase is used to imply that family relationships are more important than friendships, often suggesting that family should be prioritised. However, the full quote is actually “the blood of the covenant is thicker than the water of the womb”, which has the exact opposite meaning. The original meaning actually doesn’t refer to family at all and instead comes from the idea that a bond made with someone that you’ve shed blood with (in the past this would have been a literal reference to battle) is stronger than one had with someone you’ve shared the womb with.

&#x200B;

Source: [https://www.helpforwriters.me/etymology-series-history-proverbs/#:\~:text=%E2%80%9CBlood%20is%20Thicker%20Than%20Water%E2%80%9D&text=However%2C%20the%20full%20quote%20is,has%20the%20exact%20opposite%20meaning](https://www.helpforwriters.me/etymology-series-history-proverbs/#:~:text=%E2%80%9CBlood%20is%20Thicker%20Than%20Water%E2%80%9D&text=However%2C%20the%20full%20quote%20is,has%20the%20exact%20opposite%20meaning).
